    "summary": "Gritty Jerry and fiery Brent solo; uneven but bright moments",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a gritty, youthful Jerry Garcia on \"Beat It On Down The Line\" and an angry, hellacious Brent Mydland solo. Highlights include strong performances of \"Let It Grow\" and \"Never Trust A Woman,\" while the second set stalls during a troubled \"Good Lovin'.\" Overall energy is high but the performance wavers in places.",
    "review": "What unfolds at Nassau Coliseum on April 11, 1982 is a snapshot of the Dead in a transitional mood. Jerry’s guitar work carries a soulful grit that shines especially on the driving \"Beat It On Down The Line,\" while his vocal presence lifts songs like \"Candyman\" and the opening half‑step suite. Brent Mydland steals the spotlight with an angry, hellacious organ solo that many reviewers cite as a highlight of the night, though his stage exit during \"Good Lovin'\" adds a jarring note to an otherwise lively set. The first set recovers from a shaky start to produce standout moments such as \"Let It Grow\" and the plaintive \"Never Trust A Woman,\" but lyrical slips and occasional drop‑outs—particularly on the audience tape—prevent the show from reaching consistent brilliance. The second set builds momentum with solid jams before losing steam after the problematic \"Good Lovin',\" leaving fans with a mixed impression of an evening that was both inspired and uneven.",
